Q: ASP.NET Core MVC form submission returns HTTP 405 Method Not Allowed when using attribute routing A: <p>giving each form a name and specifying what method/verb they hits in your form would fix your bug. e.g</p>
<pre><code>
&lt;form name=&quot;login&quot;&gt;&lt;/form&gt;
&lt;form name=&quot;register&quot;&gt;&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Would that be a good idea to make tuples like initializer_list of different types? A: <p>As you observed, type deduction with <code>auto</code> is not possible in your use case, unless writting <code>std::make_tuple</code> for instance. Which means that you have to help the compiler in one way or another.</p>
<p>If your intention is to store a list of arguments for a specific function, you actually have already the required type information, i.e. the types of the function’s parameters, which can be retrieved in the form of a tuple. For example, here is a (very simple) version of a type trait providing as a tuple the parameter types of a function <code>Fct</code>:</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;auto Fct&gt;
struct parameters {
    template &lt;typename R, typename...T&gt;
    static auto parametersOf(R(*)(T...)) -&gt; std::tuple&lt;T...&gt;;
    using type = decltype(parametersOf(Fct));
};
</code></pre>
<p>so you can now &quot;help&quot; the compiler by providing the actual type instead of <code>auto</code> for defining <code>regs</code></p>
<pre><code>int main() {

    const parameters_t&lt;QueryRegString&gt; regs[] = {
        {1, &quot;Windows Kits\\Installed Roots&quot;, &quot;KitsRoot10&quot;},
        {2, &quot;Windows Kits\\Installed Roots&quot;, &quot;KitsRoot81&quot;},
        {3, &quot;Microsoft SDKs\\Windows\\v7.1&quot;, &quot;InstallationFolder&quot;},
    };

    for (auto const&amp; t : regs) {
        std::apply ([](auto&amp;&amp;...args) {
            QueryRegString (std::forward&lt;decltype(args)&gt;(args)...);
        }, t);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/E35sbeE86" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>Adding explicitely <code>parameters_t&lt;QueryRegString&gt;</code> for defining <code>regs</code> is somewhat a good idea since it gives an indication of the purpose of the object itself, i.e. a list of arguments to be provided as input of a given function.</p>
<p>Finally, I would say that the idea behind the question probably concerns the proper use of <code>auto</code>, and in particular its overuse. When I first discovered <code>auto</code>, I thought it encouraged a certain laziness because, if you know the type you’re working with, why not make it explicit in the code? I have (partly) revised that initial opinion, but it seems to me that in your specific use case, explicitly stating the type as proposed above genuinely provides useful information to the reader of the code.</p>
